      "text": "We are given that Carolyn must remove a number $n,$ such that on each of her turns, Carolyn must pick a number into which she can factor and isolate a given number. In this particular problem, we see that the negative of a multiple of $2$ would work for $n=6.$ Specifically $-2,$ $-4,$ and $-6$ will each give Carolyn a positive divisor while only $-2$ will give a number into which she can factor.\n\nFor Paul's part, if the number $n$ is a prime, Paul will be able to remove all of the divisors of $n.$ On the other hand, if Carolyn has in the past moved on at least $2$ landmarks before her, she can follow the $-2$ method to help Paul roll in his removed $6.$\n\nStopping at 200 gives Carolyn's sum $10$ less than is otherwise, but the accounts are where there's that added challenge, it's best to make those accounts in 2s. Carolynn starts the game, so she removes $\\sqrt{4-2}=2.-2-4,$ reducing the sum to $2$. \n\n\nAfter that, Paul takes $-6-8=-14,$ further reducing the sum to $-12.$ Then Carolyn takes $10+12+14=36$ and within this, Douglas forms $4+6-2+8=18$ using $n=12,$ and Craig takes $10+16=26,$ reducig the total to $-12-26=-38$. by the structure of the game, if Carolyn continues to do the optimal thing, she runs out of numbers available to be removed ($(6-2=4)$). Therefore the game merely becomes a function of her own first remove.",
